With <code>apply-before-merge</code>, the pull request's own run waits for the approval, applies, and merges. The default branch then holds only what was applied, and a pull request that does not merge after its apply leaves production ahead of it, so the <code>merge</code> job fails. This is the order [https://www.runatlantis.io/ Atlantis] and [https://github.com/diggerhq/digger digger] use, and it is usually called apply-before-merge.

{{Note|title=Other names|1=
* Terrateam calls the two orders [https://docs.terrateam.io/getting-started/plan-and-apply pre-merge and post-merge].
* [https://terramate.io/rethinking-iac/mastering-terraform-workflows-apply-before-merge-vs-apply-after-merge Terramate] and [https://cloudposse.com/blog/apply-after-merge Cloud Posse] call the default order apply-after-merge, and Terramate also merge-before-apply.
* After Atlantis, it is also called the Atlantis-style workflow, as in [https://feedback.spacelift.io/p/stack-locking-doesnt-support-atlantis-style-apply-before-merge-workflow Spacelift's feedback board].
}}

A pull request behind its base may be planned and applied unless <code>require-up-to-date</code> is <code>"true"</code>. The plan runs on the merge commit, so a change on the base to the same workspace makes the fresh plan differ and stops the apply, and a change to another workspace does not.

<syntaxhighlight lang="yaml" copy>
name: tofu
on:
  pull_request:
  workflow_dispatch:
    inputs:
      workspace:
        description: The workspace to apply from this branch, once its environment approves.
        required: true
        type: choice
        options: &workspaces [network, dns]
permissions: {}
jobs:
  plan:
    name: ${{ matrix.workspace }} plan
    if: github.event_name == 'pull_request'
    strategy:
      fail-fast: false
      matrix:
        workspace: *workspaces
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      contents: read
      pull-requests: write
    steps:
      - uses: femiwiki/terraform-github-tacos@v0.4.0  # x-release-please-version
        with:
          step: plan
          workspace: ${{ matrix.workspace }}

      - uses: actions/checkout@9c091bb21b7c1c1d1991bb908d89e4e9dddfe3e0  # v7.0.0
        with:
          persist-credentials: false

      - uses: dflook/tofu-plan@c7416238983bdd94c5486fb856bb7358f2bcc935  # v3.1.0
        id: plan
        env:
          GITHUB_TOKEN: ${{ github.token }}
        with:
          path: ${{ matrix.workspace }}

      - uses: femiwiki/terraform-github-tacos@v0.4.0  # x-release-please-version
        with:
          step: changes
          workspace: ${{ matrix.workspace }}
          changes: ${{ steps.plan.outputs.changes }}
          plan-json: ${{ steps.plan.outputs.json_plan_path }}
          plan-text: ${{ steps.plan.outputs.text_plan_path }}

      - uses: femiwiki/terraform-github-tacos@v0.4.0  # x-release-please-version
        if: always()
        with:
          step: collapse

  pending:
    needs: plan
    if: >-
      !cancelled() && (github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch'
      || (needs.plan.result == 'success' && github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name == github.repository))
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      actions: write
      checks: read
      pull-requests: read
    outputs:
      workspaces: ${{ steps.pending.outputs.workspaces }}
    steps:
      - uses: femiwiki/terraform-github-tacos@v0.4.0  # x-release-please-version
        id: pending
        with:
          step: pending
          apply-before-merge: "true"
          workspaces: ${{ inputs.workspace && format('["{0}"]', inputs.workspace) || '' }}

  apply:
    name: ${{ matrix.workspace }} apply
    needs: pending
    if: needs.pending.outputs.workspaces != '[]'
    strategy:
      fail-fast: false
      matrix:
        workspace: ${{ fromJSON(needs.pending.outputs.workspaces) }}
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    environment: ${{ matrix.workspace }}
    permissions:
      actions: read
      contents: read
      pull-requests: write
    steps:
      - uses: femiwiki/terraform-github-tacos@v0.4.0  # x-release-please-version
        id: approver
        with:
          step: apply
          workspace: ${{ matrix.workspace }}
          apply-before-merge: "true"

      - uses: actions/checkout@9c091bb21b7c1c1d1991bb908d89e4e9dddfe3e0  # v7.0.0
        with:
          persist-credentials: false

      - uses: dflook/tofu-apply@e3e5dfc8f935d4b152c767801046f63abf8a77d3  # v3.1.0
        id: apply
        env:
          GITHUB_TOKEN: ${{ github.token }}
        with:
          path: ${{ matrix.workspace }}
          auto_approve: ${{ github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch' }}

      - uses: femiwiki/terraform-github-tacos@v0.4.0  # x-release-please-version
        if: always()
        with:
          step: result
          workspace: ${{ matrix.workspace }}
          outcome: ${{ steps.apply.outcome }}
          plan-json: ${{ steps.apply.outputs.json_plan_path }}
          plan-text: ${{ steps.apply.outputs.text_plan_path }}
          approver: ${{ steps.approver.outputs.approver }}

  # Make this the one required check
  gate:
    name: ${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' && 'tofu gate' || 'tofu gate (pull requests only)' }}
    needs: [plan, pending, apply]
    if: always() && github.event_name == 'pull_request'
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions: {}
    steps:
      - uses: femiwiki/terraform-github-tacos@v0.4.0  # x-release-please-version
        with:
          step: gate
          needs: ${{ toJSON(needs) }}

  merge:
    needs: [pending, apply, gate]
    if: >-
      !cancelled() && needs.gate.result == 'success'
      && (needs.apply.result == 'success' || needs.pending.outputs.workspaces == '[]')
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      actions: read
      contents: write
      deployments: read
      pull-requests: write
    steps:
      - uses: femiwiki/terraform-github-tacos@v0.4.0  # x-release-please-version
        with:
          step: merge
          apply-before-merge: "true"
</syntaxhighlight>

== Stacked pull requests ==
In a [https://docs.github.com/en/pull-requests/get-started/about-stacked-prs native stack], which GitHub has in public preview, approving a pull request does what merging it does: the bottom one applies and merges alone, and one higher up applies its own plan, which carries every pull request below it, and merges the whole stack. Higher up, <code>pending</code> fails when that plan changes more than one workspace, since their applies run side by side and one may need what another has yet to apply. Apply such a stack from the bottom: once the lowest pull request merges, GitHub retargets the next one at the default branch, and its next run plans it from there.

A pull request that targets another pull request's branch without being in a native stack is never applied.
